# Avoiding Memory Exhaustion When Counting Lines in Large Files

The error `Fatal error: Allowed memory size of 268435456 bytes exhausted` is a classic symptom of trying to load an entire file into RAM before processing it. In your specific case, the culprit is `$lines = count(file($path)) - 1;`. The `file()` function returns an array containing every single line from the path provided to it. When you pass a 500MB text file to this function, PHP must allocate contiguous memory blocks to store the content of that file in an array structure. Even if the lines are short, the overhead of the array keys and string objects pushes the total memory usage significantly higher than the raw file size.

Once the script attempts to count this massive array, it hits the `memory_limit` directive (usually set to 256MB by default on many shared hosting environments or local setups). This is why you see the error occurring at line 109, right after trying to allocate just 440 bytes; the engine has already failed because the previous allocation of the entire file content exceeded the limit.

To solve this for files ranging from 2MB to 1GB and beyond, you need to switch from a bulk-load strategy to a stream-based approach. The most efficient method in PHP is using `file_get_contents()` combined with a regex match, or better yet, iterating over the file pointer without loading it entirely if you are processing multiple files in a loop. However, for a single pass count where performance is paramount, reading the file as a single string and counting newline characters is often faster than opening the file handle repeatedly, provided you have enough memory to hold that single string.

A robust solution involves reading the file into a string and using `substr_count`. This avoids creating an array of lines, which saves significant memory overhead associated with PHP's internal data structures for strings and arrays. Here is how you can refactor your code:

```php
$path = 'your_large_file.txt';
$content = @file_get_contents($path);

if ($content === false) {
    throw new RuntimeException('Failed to open file');
}

// Count occurrences of the newline character (\n) or Windows-style (\r\n)
$lineCount = substr_count($content, "\n") + substr_count($content, "\r");
```

This approach loads the entire file into one string variable. While this still uses RAM proportional to the file size, it eliminates the massive overhead of an array. A 500MB file might consume around 600â700MB of RAM with this method due to PHP's internal string representation, which is generally safe on systems with 2GB+ available memory and a relaxed `memory_limit`. If your environment strictly enforces 256MB, you cannot count lines for files larger than that threshold using in-memory methods regardless of the technique.

If your server has strict memory limits (e.g., 128MB or 256MB) and you are dealing with gigabyte-sized files, you must process the file incrementally. You can use `fopen()` to get a file handle and read chunks, but counting newlines in chunks is tricky because you need to handle edge cases where a newline falls exactly at the boundary of two reads. A safer incremental approach involves checking the last byte of each chunk to ensure you aren't double-counting or missing partial lines.

```php
$handle = fopen($path, 'r');
if ($handle === false) {
    throw new RuntimeException('Failed to open file');
}

$lines = 0;
$lastChar = '';

while (!feof($handle)) {
    $chunk = fread($handle, 8192); // Read in 8KB chunks
    if ($lastChar === "\n") {
        $lines++;
    } elseif (substr($chunk, 0, 1) === "\n") {
        $lines++;
    }
    $lastChar = substr($chunk, -1);
}

fclose($handle);
echo $lines;
```

This pattern ensures that you only count a newline if it completes the line within the current context, effectively handling file boundaries correctly. The trade-off here is slight CPU overhead compared to `substr_count`, but it guarantees zero memory growth beyond the size of the read buffer (8KB in this example). For scripts processing gigabytes of data on constrained hosts, this is the only viable path.

Alternatively, if you are running this logic within a Laravel application and have access to higher resource limits or can adjust the `memory_limit` directive in your PHP configuration (`php.ini`), sticking to the `file_get_contents` method is usually preferred due to its simplicity and speed. If you need to share this logic with a team using the [LaravelCompany](https://laravelcompany.com) ecosystem, ensuring that your worker processes have sufficient RAM allocated is just as important as writing efficient code.
